            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> The `on_key` calback only gets called **after** evaluating the mappings. And the keys are indeed the evaluated keys.\r\n> \r\n> What we would need to do it properly, is:\r\n> \r\n> * an event when a mapping has been completed (including internal key binding without a mappping like `viw`)\r\n> \r\n> * a callback that is triggered on every single key that was pressed by the user\r\n\r\nYeah, quick test confirms that it is not straightforwardly usable. Shame :(\r\n\r\n> So you would just need to create a mapping that binds `<leader>` to **mini.clue**. No need to mess around with `vim.on_key`\r\n\r\nAnd also for any non-terminal `<Leader>` mappings. Having to do this is the reason I wanted to try and use `on_key()` in the first place.\r\nThe only alternative I see right now is to indeed make custom mapping only for `<Leader>` and implement own `getchar()` based logic for retrieving next keys (something along the lines of 'mini.jump2d').\r\n\r\nAnyway, 'mini.clue' is not yet even in top 3 of my priority list, so it will be a while. Besides some rare mappings-related issues, yours 'which-key.nvim' does a great job.",
